Subject: Suggestions for handling MS documents under OpenVMS
Date: 1997/03/31
Message-ID: <jeremy-3103972223430001@ppp46.adelaide.on.net.au>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 229776499
Followup-To: comp.os.vms
Organization: VSM Software Services Pty Ltd
Reply-To: jeremy@vsm.com.au
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


Hi,

For years now I've been using my VAXstation to handle my e-mail and word
processing needs.  I run Innosoft's PMDF mail software and DECwrite.

A situation has now developed where I need to able to read & generate
documents for MS Word and MS Excel, and I would like to hear how others
have dealt with this -- short of buying a PC.

The main requirement is to be able to read MS Word documents received by e-mail.
It's possible to extract the text out of such a document, but it would be
nice to be able to convert it to something I can process locally and
preserve text attributes, etc.  I've been looking into the CDA Converter
Library and although it has some nice features the SPD is dated 1994 and
it looks like DEC hasn't touched it since then.  The SPD says it will
process MS Word documents, but only up to Word 5, and the documents I'm
being given are from Word 6 & 7.

Does anyone know who is now looking after CDA Converter Library, or is it
a dead product?  Is there an alternative product?  I quite like the idea
of this approach because whatever converter I buy can be slotted into PMDF
so that when I get to read the mail it comes up as a DDIF document (I can
even configure it to start DECwrite automatically).

Another approach would be to run SoftPC on the VAXstation and to install
the MS products into that.  This should work but again the SoftPC product
doesn't seem to have kept up to date with things: it emulates only a 286
and the SPD implies it supports only Windows 3.1 (not 3.11) or DOS.  Has
Digital dropped this one too?

I should conclude by raising a third possibility.  I am about to order an
AlphaStation 255/300 (running OpenVMS).  I have noticed that there are
PCI-based co-processor cards available for the Macintosh.  Does anyone
know of a similar card (with associated software) which can be plugged
into my new system?

Please reply via e-mail if at all possible as I don't read Usenet NEWS as
often as I would like!

Thanks,

