References: <5hdko0$ajg@news-rocq.inria.fr> <333A934D.7688@jpmorgan.com>
Organization: University of Colorado
Newsgroups: comp.benchmarks,comp.lang.fortran,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.hp.misc,comp.arch



For our large cluster of Alpha systems, we're currently using Fast
Ethernet as the interconnect. Granted, 8MB/s using TCP/IP aint much,
but we can scale to a large number of nodes, and it's very cheap. We
used the Bay 28115 FE switchs, which run about $7500 or so for 15 FE
ports. The FE cards are ~$150. Total interconnect cost is ~$620, and I
had already factored the cost of the FE card into the base system
cost.

We also have memory channel between two nodes (it's fast, but
currently limited to 8 nodes). We have Myrinet (www.myri.com) between
four other nodes. This is fast and scalable, but not terribly robust
(yet).


-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



