First let's make sure it is understood that I was reporting
performance of the executable, i.e. 2.29 is better than 1.39.

I always try to get the compiler/hardware maker to pick the flags.
The conditions were:  DEC C X5.6-043 
flags:  -std1 -O5 -migrate -non_shared -tune -ev5 -fast 

The alpha tests were done on DEC machines by DEC representatives.
Burkhard did not report back the gcc flags this time, but he has
lots of experience with optimization on both compilers, and he was
trying to convince me to buy a Linux alpha system.  On another
benchmark with a similar memory/cpu mix, we found that no option
we could throw at gcc gave the performance of the DEC compilers.



