Fredrik Nyman <ice@abattoir.gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:

>[...]
>So why does a motherboard (Digital PC164, whish is
>pretty simple) and a 533 MHz 21164PC CPU typically sell for $2,700 or
>so?  The motherboard shouldn't cost more than $200 or so in OEM
>quantities, right?  

The $2700 figure is for a +complete+ system, including 17" monitor,
not just for the CPU and motherboard.

In a presentation back in October, Digital gave the following
est. cost breakdown of a 233MHz Klamath v. a 21164PC:
 
Subsystem                           Klamath-233 21164PC
Microprocessor/Chipset/Cache RAM        475     425
Motherboard                             143     239
32MB EDO/S DRAM                         160     160
Graphics64-bit/2MB      SG               55      55
2.4GB Hard-disk Drive                   160     160
Floppy, 8x CD-ROM Drive                  61      61
Case/Power Supply                        40      80
17inch  Monitor                         360     360
Keyboard & Mouse                         17      17
Windows NT,Software,Packaging           203     203
Total Cost                             1674    1760
Gross Margin (30%)                      502     528
Total System Price                     2176    2288
Estimated SPECint95/SPECfp95            9/7   14/17

It's worth noting that the PC164 motherboard is for use with the
21164a chip, not the 21164PC. Digital is developing a new motherboard, 
the 164SX, for the 21164PC.

