Fredrik Nyman <ice@abattoir.gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:
> ...
> > The $2700 figure is for a +complete+ system, including 17" monitor,
> > not just for the CPU and motherboard.
> Nope.  I was quoted a price of $2,700 by one of Digital's
> resellers (http://www.dcginc.com) just last Friday:
>....
> By the way, how do the 21164a and the 21164PC differ?
--

You're comparing apples & oranges.

The 21164A motherboard and CPU you quoted is probably the PC164 or
EB164 motherboard, which is available now and allows you to build a
system around it.

The 21164PC is a vairant of the 21164, but is currently not shipping.
It is expected to be available in 2Q97 or 3Q97. The $2700 as a
complete package includes 30% gross margin - i.e., the nominal list
price is $2700.

The 21164PC differs from the 21164A by not having the 96KByte level-2
on-chip cache. I also think that the I-cache is 16Kbytes and D-cache
is 8-Kbytes. It also have the moving video extensions.  The resulting
chip has smaller die area than the 21164, and from what I hear,
smaller die area (& ideally better yields) than the "Pentium-II"
(kalamath).



-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



