One sidelight about DFS...
Digital has had a distributed wide area filesystem for years, namely the
built-in access via DECnet (and in 7.1, in tcp/ip, though not as universally).
Servers exist for a number of OSs and have for ages (DECnet was released just
before SNA was, and was published free as an effort to provide an industry
standard for peer to peer networking which anyone can implement, without
paying a cent for licenses to anyone else. DECnet has always been like that,
though Digital's implementations have long been the most widespread and
arguably the best (through greatest exposure and long use).

The existence of that, of DEC's DFS product (a faster vms-to-vms version of
distributed filesystems), and of nfs client and server implementations has
tended to reduce the interest in DCE DFS in the OVMS space. The notion of
having one's filesystem being all over a wide area network has been in OVMS
for years before NFS even existed, with widely understood security properties
and implications.

Thus it is to a degree unsurprising that the DCE DFS is not asked for widely.

There are a good many other things which are widely needed...
Glenn Everhart



