Ling Wang <wang_ling@jpmorgan.com> writes:

> 
> Robert Harley wrote:
> > You can buy a
> > 500Mhz/64MB/4GB Alpha for *half* what your dual PPro costs, and the
> > Alpha will "blow the doors off" it (in fact if you were to shell out
> > for a big L3 cache it would typically match a quad PPro for serious
> > number-crunching).  "Off hand" twenty of these would be a much better bet.
> 
> Lets not forget, put the 20 machines in the guy's house.  Build
> a extra room w/rack for 20 boxes w/their monitor.  A/C to cool
> the room & custom 100AMP line to power all of them.  Hub to 
> connect all the boxes.  Extra cost?  few hundred extra K's.  
> And what do you get?  A totally unmanagable network where
> you run back and forth between machines constantly(if you
> have NT) and your numbers still wouldn't crunch because of
> all the network badwidth and latencies.

Well, you're right that you have to factor in the cost of the
interconnect, but a "few hundred extra K's" is a bit exagerated.  Right
now, DEC's best interconnect is Memory Channel, which has 10usec latency
and 65MB bandwidth.  This is quite adequate for most HPF applications.
Last I heard, Memory Channel runs about $5K per node.  Four of these and
Four fast Alpha workstations would easily fit into your budget and leave
plenty of money left over for software.  DEC has the best HPF (speedup
typically scales linearly with the number of processors), which means your
Fortran code can use the whole cluster without writing any explicit
message-passing code.  The runtime environment for HPF is pretty simple to
configure, and relatively low maintenance (no "running back and forth
between machines").  You would only need one nice monitor--you could
handle everything from there.

500Mhz machines, plus 4x speedup from HPF?  Sounds pretty nice to me!

-Israel Gale
