Subject: Final results of portable benchmark for single, SMP and MPP computers
Date: 1997/03/28
Message-ID: <333C2162.6779@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 229070490
Organization: University of Minnesota
Newsgroups: comp.benchmarks,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.intel


Hi!

Here are the final results for the benchmark that many
people have run on their computer systems. The code in
all cases produces the same results, but there are three
main versions:

- ANSI C version
- Multithreaded SMP NT version (2, 4, or 8 threads)
- Cray T3D/T3E version (#nodes = 2^n, where n > 0)

The C code also has a version which works well on
non-Intel machines (i.e., assumes many fp registers).
The code is very fp and memory intensive. Rob Harley
rewrote the critical routines for the RISC version,
and his latest tweaks are only reflected in the
results for his 500MHz Alpha, and the T3E. Only a 
single CPU was used for the SGI run... it would
have to be ported for that system, but I do not
have access to one, do I couldn't do it.

The code is a full QCD code, which performs 5 iterations
of the Hybrid Monte Carlo method on a 8^4 lattice,
with dynamical staggered quarks.

I am sorry I didn't also collect all the important
information, such as cache/memory details (which
are obviously important). I am working on a 
completely new algorithm, which will be easier to
use, and will also be more portable to other systems.
Thanks to everyone who sent me the information!

All the following machines produced the same
output:

                                            Mflops

Cray T3E (4x375MHz 21164, 8K L1, 96K L2)  : 317 (79*4)
HP K460 1-way (180Mhz PA-8000)            : 260
tweaked code on 500MHz Alpha              : 209
quad ppro 200Mhz/512KB                    : 205 (52*4)
500MHz Alpha (21164)                      : 177
400Mhz Alpha(feedback-driven-optimization): 173
SGI Origin2000 (single CPU)               : 148
dual ppro 233MHz/512K                     : 125 (63*2)
SGI Origin 200, 180 MHz, 1 MB L2          : 123 
Alpha 433 Mhz (21164)(Linux)              : 113
333MHz Alpha (21164)                      : 113
Dell 200Mhz dual ppro (256k cache)        : 109 (55*2)
266MHz Alpha (21164)                      : 101
167 MHz Sun Ultra 1                       :  83
233 MHz VMS/Alpha (21064A)                :  68
Same ppro2, but on single CPU             :  67 
P6-200/256K Asus P6NP5 Linux 2.0.18       :  67
single-PPro 180 Mhz                       :  58 
Dell p133c (512k cache)                   :  34
Sun Ultra 1 143Mhz Solaris 2.5.1          :  32
PowerMac 7100/66 (66mhz) 256K L2 (MacOS)  :  28
Sun SS20/61 (SuperSPARC, 50/60, 1M+20/16) :  25
Cray T3D (1 node)                         :  24 
PowerMac 7100/66 (66mhz) 256K L2 (Linux)  :  19
Vaxstation 4000/90, VMS 6.2               :  12
486DX2/66 Linux 2.0.29                    :   4
4 processor(40mhz) SUN 690 (1 CPU)        :   3
Sinclair ZX-81                            :   0

It is not easy to compare the Cray T3E (4 nodes) results
with the others, since the subgrid size on each node
may be different. Of course, imagine the T3E running this
code on 2048 nodes.... on a much larger lattice, 
of course.

cheers

Henley



