>No, you need to compare dollar for dollar.
>[...]
>I can tell you, $7K will buy a top-quality, completely kick-ass dual
>Pentium Pro system that you can load down with a tremendous number of
>number-crunching users.  Off hand, ten of those may be the best bet.
>Any one of these killer micros would blow the doors off an old Cray.

That sound like a really bad deal to me.  You can buy a
500Mhz/64MB/4GB Alpha for *half* what your dual PPro costs, and the
Alpha will "blow the doors off" it (in fact if you were to shell out
for a big L3 cache it would typically match a quad PPro for serious
number-crunching).  "Off hand" twenty of these would be a much better bet.


>And besides, you can get a better operating system for Intel machines.

Since you refer to "a tremendous number of number-crunching users" you
must not mean Windows 95.  If you mean Linux or Windows NT, they run
on the Alphas too.  And besides, you can get better operating systems
for Alphas, namely VMS and Digital Unix.  The latter is the best of
all but a tad expensive.  Quite a big tad unfortunately :(

