Robert Harley <harley@pauillac.inria.fr> wrote:

>Since you refer to "a tremendous number of number-crunching users" you
>must not mean Windows 95.  If you mean Linux or Windows NT, they run
>on the Alphas too.  And besides, you can get better operating systems
>for Alphas, namely VMS and Digital Unix.  The latter is the best of
>all but a tad expensive.  Quite a big tad unfortunately :(


I'm not so sure Digital Unix is "better" than Linux.  At least not
for what I do with the computer.

We have machines around here which run both Linux/alpha and D-UX:
The Linux binaries are smaller
The Linux machine apparently shares memory better, so that I can run
  about 30% more stuff on the machine than under D-UX
  (i.e., better usage of RAM)
The Linux machine will run D-UX binaries
Redhat has the nice rpm facility

Some downsides to the Linux machine:
NFS is slower 
Some little things don't work (utmp/wtmp seems hozed)
Linux can be more difficult to install.
It might be difficult to obtain the best optimizing compilers
  for Linux.

The jury is out on stability, but the Linux box hasn't crashed
since we got it properly installed.

PeterM



