In article <5he7a7$ofu@client2.news.psi.net>, genanr@thumper2.emsphone.com (Andrew N. Ryan) writes:
> 	I would like to hear from anybody that has experience with clustering
> ALPHA systems.  We are going to upgrade our current Oracle system (currently
> a quad pentium 66 system running SCO (yuck!)) and are looking into our
> options.  I would like to know what experiences people have had running
> Oracle on an ALPHA cluster.  What kind of inter node bandwidth do you get? 
> How much slower is it compared to true SMP?  The main reason I'm asking
> about clustering is I was hoping to cluster two dual processing ALPHA clones
> rather that pay DEC what they want for a for processor system.  We currently
> have a true DEC ALPHA with 2 processors (exp. to 4) but it's used for a
> special project (using Redbrick). If we do decide to cluster we would 
> probably look into adding that system into the cluster also.  Any other
> information about this would be useful.  Thanks.

Alphas can be clustered using a variety of media: CI, Ethernet,
Memory Channel (reflective memory), FDDI, DSSI and probably ATM
by now.  All questions about speed, bandwidth and latency are
highly dependent upon which combination of those you choose.

How Oracle will behave depends on whether you are using Oracle 7
(or are they up to 8?) or Oracle Rdb, since they make use of the
cluster in different fashions.

If you do a thorough job of reading specifications, non-DEC hardware
should not be much of an issue, so long as it supports the interface
types you have chosen.

If you choose Unix or Windows NT, your choices will be more
restricted than the above, which is the full set supported
on VMS.

Larry Kilgallen



