Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Ron K. wrote:
> 
> I have the Starion 911 desktop computer.  The one-year warranty is over,
> so I don't want to call Digital and get charged.  I recently had to
> reformated my HD, and I tried to reinstall the Restore disk that came
> with the computer.  (This restores all the programs that was on it when
> I it was new.)  I reboot using the soft disk, which runs a program
> giving me the option to overwrite the HD and restore programs.  The
> autoexec then looks for programs (using a recovery.crc file I had on
> backup) that are no longer on my HD because I already wiped them out
> (and I don't have a backup of).  I then get an error message saying that
> it couldn't reinstall the programs and reboots the computer.
> 
> What's going on, and how do I reinstall the programs on my HD so it
> looks like it did when I bought the computer?  I figure this must be
> some kind of prevention for installing the programs on other HD's, but
> what do I do when my HD was wiped out before I could run the Recovery
> program?

The restore process is a two step operation:

1. format the hard drive
2. Install the software

See if you can determine where the error message is taking place, during
format and during the restore. You can do this if you use the F8
function key during boot and single step through the process. If you are
getting the error during the format step, you may have a problem with
the disk. Make sure that you use DOS 6.22 versions of FDISK and FORMAT
on the HD prior to running the restore. The restore uses FORMAT from DOS
6.22 and needs the disk to have been FDISK'ed with the same version., If
you had used a different version of FDISK, you will get an error while
trying to format.

If the error is happening after the format, it could be that there is
still a problem with the disk. The restore is basically taking a large
ZIP (that is a complete image of the original software install), copying
the ZIP to the HD and upzipping. Once the unzip has taken place, there
is a compare program (the CRC) that checks each file for integrity. If
all the files check out, then the restore process completes and you can
reboot and start the Win95 install.

-- 
