In article <5h9k4m$1b8@caterpillar.demon.co.uk>, william@caterpillar.demon.co.uk (W.E. Hargrove) writes:

:Can any one tell what the above 'hardware??' is in the subject heading.
:Any info. that people can provide me with, or pointers to info would be
:gratefully received.

   Uh, you already know pretty much everything about this box.

   It's an old and slow VAXstation 3100 series processor -- the KA42-A
   is the processor module used in the VAXstation 3100 models 30 and 40,
   while the KA42-B is the processor used on the VAXstation 3100 models
   38 and 48.  The -40 and -48 variants incorporate additional storage
   peripheral bays inside the enclosure, but are otherwise similar to
   the -30 and -38, respectively.

   These systems are supported under the OpenVMS operating system
   (including the current OpenVMS VAX V7.1 release), and under various
   versions of the ULTRIX operating system.

   These systems support various SCSI storage peripherals, though the
   system disk is forever limited to 1.073 GB or less maximum capacity.
   (Data disks, under V6 and later OpenVMS releases, are limited to 1 TB.)

   It probably has an Ethernet connection for thinwire or thickwire,
   and it probably has a GPX-class graphics controller capable of
   1024 x 864 x 60 Hz synch-on-green.  Most DIGITAL video monitors
   support this resolution and support the synch-on-green signalling.

   The video, keyboard, and mouse interfaces predate the wide acceptance
   of the "industry standard" PC interface -- one cannot swap any of the
   "user-I/O" widgets with PC widgets.

