> Ethernet? Or is it a hardware protocol like Ethernet? (I've heard it
> described as a "intentionally non-compatible breakage of Ethernet").I will not break up your question to cause a context change...
DECNet was originally DEC's networking protocol.
It predates OSI and really predates TCP/IP by a bit.  It is not as old as 
Arpanet Host IMP Protocol (AHIP).
DDCMP, I won't break out that acronym, predates DECNet.  DECNet can run 
over DDCMP, IP, Ether, Synchronous ports using DDCMP OR TCP/IP, 
asynchronous ports, parallel ports, and all the derivatives.
You can encapsulate DECnet using CMU-IP, a good router product,
or any other means you can imagine.
It is not, was not, can't be, an intentioanl anything to do with 
Ethernet.  DECNet predates the xerox PARC efforts with Ether.
Stu Wecker was the stuckee as the spokesman, front man, hero, villan, or 
whatever came out later, of DECNet cause in the meeting with George 
Friend, Bob Rosenbaum, Clarise Patton, Harvey Schlesinger, AND MANY 
others, he did not jump back fast enough when we said we needed someone 
to work this decnet thing full time.  Stu went on to make a few bucks 
with his own company and did a lot good stuff with other networking 
things.  The rest of that crew is lurking, making waves in other places, 
or still lurking in DEC,
er sorry, digital.
 
