[...]

> I can tell you, $7K will buy a top-quality, completely kick-ass dual
> Pentium Pro system that you can load down with a tremendous number of
> number-crunching users.  Off hand, ten of those may be the best bet.
> Any one of these killer micros would blow the doors off an old Cray.

The answer to the second question first... Maybe a Cray 1....

Now the first one.  

As an owner of a linux based laptop and the creator of several 
PPro based desktops for my old research group, I can tell you that
what you indicated is in fact not true in general.

My code, a large ab-initio molecular dynamics code takes 250 seconds
per time step on a PPro 200.  

It takes 52 seconds on an old R8000 75 Mhz Power I2.  

It takes ~20 seconds on an Octane/Origin system.

The C90/YMP hasnt been tested with this new code.

Why would I want to give up 10x performance on this code in order to
save a measly 10k$?

A dual processor Origin 200 which kicks the doors off the
PPro 200 for this code runs about 25k$ educational.  This is
with about 1/4 gig of ram and 10 GB disk.

For the 64 atom runs that I indicate above, this is nice.

I just did a 512 atom run that took 8.5 hours per time step.
I couldnt imagine how many days I would wait for the PPro
to do this.

The point is that you use the box you need to use.  If you 
do real serious number crunching, then some heavy metal to
crunch them digits on is a pretty good idea.  If you want 
to use a pocket calculator to calculate general relativity
framework geodesics for orbital trajectories... go right ahead.

> Now there may be some cases where you need the mega interconnect and
> huge-shared-memory model of the Origins.  A super-high-end database
> server is a good example.  But if you really don't need that sort of
> thing, going with them is pouring most of your money down the drain.

Anytime you crunch numbers seriously hard, you need the ability
to get them to and from the CPU quickly.  John's STREAMS numbers
for Origins are nice.  They show that as you need more resources
thrown at the problem (for bigger problems) you get more resources,
with little bottlenecking effects.  Moreover, single cpu to single
cpu, the Origins crank out some seriously awesome numbers for
memory access.  Better than the "super PC" by quite a bit.
 
> And besides, you can get a better operating system for Intel machines.

Linux is awesome.  Installation was great.  The networking 
kinda sucks rocks (on speed).

I still like IRIXs look and feel better though.  

4Dwm on my laptop is my goal... :-)

The Octane is a truely awesome box.  I was just playing with it to get
VMD and other nice tools running.  VMD under Linux with Mesa just
cannot compare in speed of the visual display to the Octane.

Then again, I wouldnt want to put the octane on my lap or try to lug
that puppy around... :-)

Use the right tool for the right job.  PCs have their uses, so do
workstations and supers.

