Mark Atwood <zot@ampersand.com> wrote:

>
>Just what is DECnet?

A marketing term for the entire suite of networking components developed by
Digital.  Each major set of features releases were dubbed a "Phase".

DECnet Phase I was introduced in the mid-70s.
DECnet Phase II was out in the late 70s.
These were based on point-to-point serial data links, typically DDCMP.
DECnet Phase III was introduced with Ethernet support.
DECnet Phase IV introduced dynamic vector routing with hierarchy.
DECnet Phase V rolled out with OSI support in many layers.

DECnet spans all layers of the classic OSI reference model.
It supports many data link layers: serial, parallel, LANs or all types.
The routing and transport layers are proprietary but published as technical
documents.
DECnet also includes a number of helper and applications protocols such as:
MOP - for boot and maintenance operations
NCP - Network control Protocol
DAP - Data Access Protocol for file xfer and record access
CTERM - Terminal operation protocol
LAT - Local Area Terminal protocol (while technically not of the same design
roots... it is a part of the umbrella)

>And how interoperable is it between versions of VAX/VMS?  I've got a
>bunch of files on a VAX 11/750 running VMS 4.2 that I am going to have
>to transfer (hopefully via a network) to a uVAX 3100/96 running
>OpenVMS 7.1.

you should have almost no problems since most of those versions support DECnet
Phase IV or have backwards compatibility.
>
>What kind of headaches do I have ahead of me?
>

