References: <5g1vb9$bt4@svna0001.clipper.ssb.com> <werme.858897040@alingo.zk3.dec.com> <5gtbgi$hni@engnews2.Eng.Sun.COM> <5gu4vu$8hk$1@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>
Organization: Sponsored account, Computing Services, Carnegie Mellon, Pittsburgh, PA
Newsgroups: comp.sys.sun.admin,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.unix.solaris,comp.sys.misc,comp.sys.dec


Excerpts from netnews.comp.unix.solaris: 21-Mar-97 Re: NFS: Sun or DEC
(asking) David Robinson@jetsun.en (926)

> >But there are studies that show AFS (and I imagine Transarc DFS) gives
> >10 times the network performance.

> Be careful when reading those studies.  Most are very old and
> are based on slow machines or concentrate on performance of
> rarely used operations (create, mkdir).  AFS and DFS has advantages
> but I doubt that you will find an overall 10X network performance
> speedup.  Peak SPEC SFS (aka LADDIS) numbers show NFS servers doing
> over 24,000 operations/sec over 12 100mbps Ethernets which is likely
> to represent anywhere from 5000-15000 clients.

Indeed, be careful.  A higher-level goal for AFS and DFS is to to
*reduce* the network (and server) load of each client running an
application, as much as is possible, rather than relying on extra-fast
networks and servers.  This is intended to allow smaller/fewer servers
to support many clients, and so forth.

Ops/second numbers really aren't comparable between AFS/DFS and NFS,
impressive as the NFS server numbers might be.

		Craig



