You can tell if lat is running in a couple of ways:
run sys$system:latcp

at the prompt "sho node" or "show char"
Make sure LAT$LINK is connected

Sometimes lat doesn't start if a device isn't available. Your ethernet
device corresponds to your circuit or line in NCP
mcr ncp sho kn line

You should get back something like ewa0: or Isa0:
Remember the xxx0: is the template device and the LATACP will create
it's own device like ewa1: 
$ sho dev ew* (or whatever your specific device is)
LATACP should be connected to a device. 

Sometimes running @sys$manager:netconfig and reinitializing the executor
is enough to get it running.

Also it is important in VMS version later than 5.5 that scsnode and
scssystemid are defined in SYSGEN
$ mcr sysgen sho scsnode

If they are not set put them in your sys$system:modparams.dat file amd
either run autogen or define them in sysgen as follows:

mcr sysgen
SYSGEN> USE CURRENT
SYSGEN> SET SCSNODE NODENAME
SYSGEN> SET SCSSYSTEMID 9999 ( WHERE 9999 IS 1024 * THE AREA + NUMBER)
                             (DECNET NODE ADDRESS = AREA.NUMBER)
				(EX: IF ADDR = 1.100, THEN  ID = 1124)
SYSGEN> WRITE CURRENT
SYSGEN> EXIT
 then reboot (@sys$system:shutdown)

If all else fails 
$ def /system lat$device ewa0: ( or your device) 
then call sys$startup:lat$startup

good luck
Tony



