As I've been told (tending myself to try to use only the native stuff for
NT work, so I have not tried this personally) pc-dcl works pretty well
on the NT MSDOS windows. That's available free on places like ftp.wku.edu
on some vms sig tapes and so on. If you're in one of those msdos windows,
you get the scripting and so forth and if looking, might as well look first
at something free that is reportedly pretty widely used. A commercial
package won't be distributable and may not exist on other machines you
want to move your stuff to after you get it working.

One of the nice things of DCL on VMS is that anyone who has VMS is guaranteed
to have it. With PC-DCL on Microsoft OSs, that can also be guaranteed, by
distributing it. With something that costs, that won't be true, so your
work will be less generally usable. To my mind this vastly reduces the
value of a non-free solution. (This would be lessened if a run-only
version existed that could be distributed, but in the case of a PC DCL
I think this would be technically difficult.)

Glenn Everhart



