In article <gstovall-2403971241250001@crchma97.rich.bnr.ca>, "Greg Stovall" <gstovall@nortel.com> writes:

> A 200MHz 604e machine can be purchased for about $5K, and this machine has
> higher
> SpecInt95 (and similar SpecFP95) numbers as the 255/300.  I realize that
> SpecInt95
> is not a valid indicator of performance for some applications (it's not for
> ours),
> but for general applications, if you were not tied to some particular sw package
> that only ran on the Digital machine, why would you buy one?

For some situations a reason would be the ability to advance to a
faster machine in the future.  Certainly moving to a faster machine
within processor lines is easier than moving to a faster machine
across processor lines.

In the simple case, this might drive someone to purchase a low-end
machine in whatever line currently has the fastest machine.

In the more complex case, someone might try to purchase a machine
in whatever processor line is going to have the fastest machine
at whatever point in the future the individual needs the faster
machine.  Now you are into interpreting manufacturer claims for
the future, which is very scary.

Dick Sites, from DEC, for instance, predicted the Alpha systems
would get faster and faster due to actions along 3 directions:
clock speed, multiple issue, and SMP.  With the 21264 announcement
we see that advances due to multiple issue are not responsible,
but rather advances due to out-of-order execution.  That means
that choice of a machine would best be based not on the predictions
they make but on your trust in their general technical ability.

Larry Kilgallen



