>> If you need to run a 512 MB job, you are forced to write a message-
>> passing application. (Or buy more memory, or swap memory around the
>> existing machines.)
>--
>
>You missed my point.
>
>If you needed to run a 512MB job on a multiprocessor (e.g., we have 3
>AS2100's with that much memory), you couldn't afford to buy the memory
>because it uses a propritary interface.


You bought 36 machines at $5k-$7.5k each ($180k-$270k total), and you
don't think that you could afford 512 MB?  


Let's look at this a different way (giving us a whole new way to 
miss each others' points!):


We want to compare the usable memory of a cluster of 36 single-cpu
boxes with 128 MB RAM each versus the usable memory of a single 36-cpu
SMP machine with 36*128=4.5 GB of RAM.

On the cluster, you can immediately subtract at least 16 MB per machine
for OS overhead.

On the cluster you are also very likely to have memory load balancing
problems, making it very difficult to pack the memory on each machine.
It is very easy to imagine that you end with with 32 MB free memory on
each machine, and no small jobs to send around to use those 36 extra
little pieces.

Even if you use all the memory on the machines, you are likely to have
a number of replicated data structures that would not be needed in a
shared memory environment.

So in this (hypothetical) case, you can easily be paying for twice as
much memory as you actually use to run your jobs.


The SMP machine, on the other hand, allows you to use all of the memory
with no extra work required for (memory) load balancing.  In addition,
if the SMP machine has a 64-bit OS (SGI, Cray, or DEC), then you can
even use the entire 4.5 GG of memory (less the single OS overhead) for
a single job.  That job can be serial or parallel, message-passing or
shared-memory.


One more time --- I am not saying that clustering is a "bad thing",
or that clustering is incapable of being effective in specific instances.
What I *am* saying is that clusters are a lot less flexible than single
image shared memory machines, and that the costs of that inflexibility
need to be accounted for in any comparison of price between SMP and
clustered solutions.

(The program development, program maintenance, and system
administration costs of clusters are also much higher than for
SMP systems, but that is a different topic....)





