X-Deja-AN: 228315732
Organization: Oxford University Computing Laboratory
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



Hello,

	I'm having some problems on an Alpha 8400 with memory
consistency between processors. I've developed some barrier
synchronisation routines that have a latency of 4 usec on the 8400
compared to 90usec for the best alternative technique that uses
semaphores (see [1] below). However, the barrier doesn't guarantee
memory consistency (so it's pretty useless ;-) as a process can have
writes that are still pending, although each of the processes have
reached a consistent state. There is a nice paper about this problem
from Digital WRL labs [2], which discusses write fences on the
alpha. Is there a system call for performing a memory fence on the
Alpha? i.e., something akin to the following operation which is
available on the T3E?

Thanks...
jon

[1] `Practical barrier synchronisation'' Jonathan M. D. Hill and David
B. Skillicorn. Technical Report 16-96, Programming Research Group,
Oxford University Computing Laboratory, August 1996. 
ftp://ftp.comlab.ox.ac.uk/pub/Documents/techpapers/Jonathan.Hill/HillSkill_barrier.ps.Z

[2] ``Shared memory consistency models: a tutorial'' S. Adve and K
Gharachorloo, Digital WRL labs, Sept 1995.



MEMORY_BARRIER(3I)             CrayLibs 2.0                     SR-3903 2.0

NAME
     MEMORY_BARRIER - Blocks subsequent memory load and store operations
     until all previous loads and stores finish

SYNOPSIS
     C:

     void _memory_barrier(void);

     Fortran:

     MEMORY_BARRIER ()

IMPLEMENTATION
     CRAY T3E systems

STANDARDS
     CF90 compiler extension to Fortran 90

     Cray Standard C compiler extension to Standard C

     Cray C++ compiler extension to Draft Standard C++

DESCRIPTION
     The MEMORY_BARRIER intrinsic subroutine guarantees that subsequent
     loads and stores do not access memory until after previous loads and
     stores finish accessing memory.

     MEMORY_BARRIER does not vectorize.  The compiler generates inline code
     to evalaute this intrinsic.  The name of this intrinsic cannot be
     passed as an argument.

SEE ALSO
     CF90 Fortran Language Reference Manual, Volume 2, publication SR-3903,
     for the printed version of this man page.

