For about DM 130000 (which is $70k range) at a former job in Bremen we
got 3 500 MHz Alphas each with a 20" monitor, 320 MB each and a total of
about 30 GB disk and a tape drive for backups as well as the usual
CD-ROM drives, mouse, keyboards, cables etc.  These are not clones but
from a Digital reseller but of course there was an academic discount and
perhaps some `special offer' discount as well.  We looked at what was
the best configuration FOR THE STUFF WE DO THERE (climate modelling---
atmosphere, ocean etc.; mostly number crunching, lots of memory, not too
much I/O) for that price from each vendor, so each `test configuration'
was different, since the money was fixed, so the number of machines,
processors per machine etc varied from vendor to vendor.  We ran our own
benchmarks which were often very different from the SPEC values and the
own/SPEC ratio varied from vendor to vendor and from test to test and
was sometimes >1, sometimes <1.  We looked at IBM, DEC, SGI, SUN and HP.
SUN and IBM were much behind the others (we were exclusively RS 6000
until we bought the Digital machines).  HP didn't have an F90 compiler
but was comparable to DEC and SGI in performance.  The battle was
between DEC and SGI, and we went for DEC because of slightly more
performance and a better Fortran implementation.  (Easy-to-understand
compiler, nice defaults, understandable options etc.  I was a bit
worried about SGI when I dragged up an old newsgroup posting from
several months before concerning a run-time error and it still wasn't
fixed in the newest version). 

If you need more power than this I would really like to know what you 
are doing with the machine.  


