Dirk Grunwald  <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> wrote:
>mccalpin@frakir.asd.sgi.com (John McCalpin) writes:
>> First, you have the problem that the memory is statically partitioned
>> between the boxes.  So if you need to use more memory than the max in
>> any one box, you are forced to write parallel (message-passing) programs.
>> (HPF does hide the message-passing from you, if you can find a vendor
>> that will sell you an HPF over MPI compiler for these boxes).
>
>We have a cluster of ~36 DEC alpha systems based on the EB164-300Mhz
>chip, each with 256 MB of memory. The total system price was
>~$5000-7500, depending on when we bought them (the 500Mhz models were
>the same price at the end, but we wanted a homogenous cluster).
>
>These systems can use commodity DRAM's, and have a reasonble bandwidth
>to individual CPU's. The multiprocessor systems need special purpose
>memory boards & controllers - much more $.

You missed my point.

If you need to run a 512 MB job, you are forced to write a message-
passing application. (Or buy more memory, or swap memory around the
existing machines.)

There is no doubt that you could get very cost-effective throughput
in this configuration (given suitable user discipline and batch queues),
and that certain message-passing jobs might run just fine.

None of this changes my point that you can't use all the stuff
that you paid for in a single shared-memory job.  

ASIDE: It may not be too long before you could get network-based,
page-oriented shared memory in such a cluster.  This might work for
some applications, but the latency for getting a page across a
switched, fast ethernet (or switched FDDI) net would be something like
1000 microseconds for a 8kB page, compared to shared-memory access
times of 0.5 microseconds on the SGI Origin 200.   Certainly, there
exist applications with coarse enough granularity to tolerate 1000
microsecond latency, but the set is a lot smaller than the set that
can tolerate 0.5 microsecond latency.


>Likewise, if you look at the PWS 500a line, you can get SDRAM systems
>with ~1GB/s memory subsystems for a similar price now. If you got 8 of
>these (say $64,000), you'd have 8GB/s of main memory bandwidth, much
>more than any single SMP is going to give you. Depending on your
>problem, you may be able to work this nicely (e.g., subdomains in
>FEM's, etc). Depends completely on your problem.

(1) I'd like to look at the PWS 500a, but I have no idea what it is.
Perhaps this is the DEC Personal WorkStation line?  (Looks like a
nice box -- if you have to run NT.)

(2) You should take claims of 1 GB/s memory systems with a great
deal of scepticism.  Although 66 MHz SDRAM systems have a nominal
burst rate of ~1000 MB/s, the sustained rate will be much lower.
The bandwidth that is relevant to your application is the sustained
bandwidth, and the STREAM benchmark is the most common way to measure
this.
	http://www.cs.virginia.edu/stream/

An example of an aggressive machine design using such a memory system
is the SGI Octane, which sustains about 350 MB/s on STREAM using 1 cpu
and 485 MB/s using 2 cpus.  These correspond to memory transfer rates
of 465 and 650 Mb/s.   

Alpha-based machines typically do not have as much latency tolerance as
MIPS-based machines, and so typically see a lower fraction of peak
bandwidth.  The new DEC personal workstation 433a may be an exception,
but note that they have to remove the third-level cache to get the
bandwidth up almost as high as the single-processor Octane result.
I don't have results yet for the 500 Mhz version.

(3) The big problem with parallelism in this setup is passing messages,
which is generally a huge cost in both development and maintenance.

(4) A second problem is the disparity between the cost of local and
remote references.  Sustained local bandwidth on these machines will
be in the 150-200 MB/s range, while node-to-node bandwidth will be
1 MB/s for ethernet, 12 MB/s for FDDI or Fast Ethernet.  If the 
network is shared, rather than switched, the bandwidth available per
cpu can easily get very small.


None of this is intended to say that you cannot be successful using
this sort of clustering approach, just that success in one area does
not imply that this is a general-purpose solution.  The roadsides are
littered with the remains of companies and projects that thought that
clustering was a cheap and easy way to supercomputing-land.  Some
succeeded, but many more attempts were eventually abandoned.
