In article <3334C9C8.243E@storm.ca>, Stan Pietkiewicz <stanp@storm.ca> writes:
|>In the interest of computing, I rescued (my wife isn't so sure...) 
|>a DECSsystem 5500 from the recyclers today....
|>        Now that I've got this thing at home, it's time to find out exactly
|>what I've got. It's a DECSystem 5500, Series BA400, Model 220QK-A9.
|>        Starting from the right side (looking from the back):
|>        

Let me say right at the start that I've only seen a DS5500 once, and that was
several months ago, so some of my notes may be in error.

|>        H7874-00 Power supply (this one's obvious). What's not so obvious 
|>is the purpose of the three sockets labelled M0, S1, and S0. Anything of
|>importance as far as operation goes?
|>

No idea.

|>        M7638 M7639 (two) These would be the CPU and memory boards, right?
|>How much memory is on these boards? There is also an RJ-11-like socket (this 
|>would be for the console, right?), an AUI and BNC connectors (switch 
|>selectable), a single digit LED dispaly, a two position slide switch, and a  
|>three position rotary switch. Between these and the power supply, there is
|>a panel with two Centronics-50 connectors.

M7638 is the I/O board; M7639 is a memory board, but I don't know which
variant of MS220 it might be. I'm sure the box I saw had an M7637 KN220 CPU
in it ... you seem to be missing that.

|>
|>        Next are two CXY08-M (M3119-YA) 8 line Async. communications cards.
|>The curious thing about these two cards is that they are physically different
|>even though the part numbers are the same. I didn't notice any rev. numbers
|>on the async boards; that likely would explain the difference.

How are they different?

|>
|>        TSV05   (M7530-PA) This card has a DIP switch on the edge, 4 LEDs and 
|>what looks like two SCSI-2 connectors, and there is a chip on the board labelled
|>Emulex. Would this be a SCSI controller? If so, would this board be capable
|>of controlling the boot disk?

TSV05 is a tape controller; it the connector may look like SCSI, but it isn't.

|>
|>        The next two cards (joined with 40 and 50 conductor cables) have me 
|>totally baffled. They're identified as: M7164 QDA Processor and M7165 QDA SDI.
|>

Disk controller for RA series disk drives. Not much use since you don't appear
to have any such drives.

|>        The KZQSA (M5976-SA) Rev C01 has two Centronics-50 connectors on it; 
|>would this be another SCSI controller?
|>

This *is* a SCSI controller.

|>        Last in the chain ( a half-height card) is a TQK70 (M7559) TK70?? tape 
|>drive controller. No tape drive in the box, though :-(. Will this machine work 
|>without one, or will it be next to impossible to load an OS without it?
|>

I don't know about loading an O/S, but you have corretly identified the board.

|>        In front, there are 4  5.25 full height storage device bays 
|>(unfortunately, they're empty. :-( ). Inside, you can see the BA431/441 
|>Storage Backplane. My guess is that the drives go in here, but a bracket/
|>cage/carrier is needed for the drives. And what would the interface be for
|>the devices on this BA431/441? There is also (at the far left) the other end
|>of the TK70 cable, so I'm presuming that this is where the tape drive fits.

That's where the TK70 goes. The other bays are for DSSI devices (maybe other
stuff might fit too, I don't know).

|>
|>	The last thing on the left is a pair of connectors; a Centronics-50
|>and what looks like a SCSI-2 (labelled Bus 0) with the other end of these
|>going to the BA431/441. When I got this thing (the kids have nick-named it Big
|>Blue Mother of a Whale) there was a cable attached to the Centronics-50, with 
|>the other end loose. This cable looks like it might go to one of the C-50 
|>connectors beside the M7638.
|>
|>        And finally, any hints for getting this thing going? Has either of NetBSD 
|>or OpenBSD been ported to this architecture (MIPS???)
|>        

There is a NetBSD port for some of the MIPS range: pmax is the one I remember.
I don't know whether DS5500 works with NetBSD.

The I/O board (M7638) supposedly handles both DSSI and SCSI drives so your
best bet seems to be to get hold of the relevant hardware manual (the part
number for which currently eludes me) and figure out how to hook up a cheap
SCSI drive to the box. The load your O/S either via the ethernet or
via a SCSI tape.

