: And how fast does electricity travel?

The electrons themselves move pretty slow compared to light but thats not what 
really matters, what matters is the speed of the wavefront of a pulse. 
This has little to do with the motion of electrons, and thw wavefront moves
at the speed of light. 

the place where optical computers hope to gain advantages are:
1. most of the time spent on a signa is on switching the state of some 
element in response ot the signal. 
2. light can be used for accessing without physically invaisve methods a solid
three dimensional storage system (e.g. holographic crystals, protien blocks, 
anything else people can dream up in the meantime) and move this data in
parallel more effectively than an electrical signal. 
3. the possibility for moving data around in planes rather in lines 
makes the bandwidth of optical computers potentially much larger than that of 
electronic computers. 

Speed of signal propagation is NOT an advantage of optical computing,
however. Actually to my knowledge the switching speed of reliable 
optical switches is slower than a GaAs IC. remember, this is the time
required for a substance to change its optical characteristics, be they 
refractive angle or opacity, and for this to happen some initiator signal
is required just as an initiator signal changes the state of a transistor. 

reaction to this signal ordinarily would be some molecular rearrangement,
which would effect the optical changes in the substance and thus
effect a switch.
A major drawback to optical computing is that while individual gates might
 be built in labs, they are large items and interesting only for theoretical
valu,e and nobody can figure out a way to make the bloody things 
a: small enough
b: ptu them together into useful machines. 

Isildur
 





