In article <333170E3.7403@teleport.com>, 

Eric Neiwert wanted to know if he could use his Windows NT server to make
a donated PrintServer 20 available to his Macs and PowerMacs without buying
Pathworks for the Macs.

I think the problem is going to be getting the PrintServer 20 to boot at all.

I don't have direct experience with the 20, but I've used the 40 and a bunch of 
the 17s, and they can be used by Macs.  (All the Printservers speak native
PostScript, although they don't have AppleTalk support built in. The NT box can
serve the printer and speak AppleTalk, so that's likely to work.  We're doing
this with our Printserver 17s, and print is getting through, although you may
need to find a PPD file for the Macs for best results.)

The problem is that the PrintServers need a boot host in order to load their
OS (VAXeln, if anybody cares; there's a VAX in that printer) and the PostScript
interpreter.  The ones we have use MOP to boot, which isn't natively supported
on anything but Digital gear as far as I know.  I think Digital has printers
now that will use bootp or tftp, but I think the 20 is old enough that it's not
supported.  In any case, Eric would need to get the boot images from someplace,
and configure one of the Linux boxes to do the load.

Hope this helps,

-- Alan

