A photon is relatively huge as compared to an electron.  Optical gates
are much larger than their electronic counter parts.  On the other hand,
a photon is considerably less massive than an electron.  As such it
dissapates less energy.  The real issue is how many and what attributes
of these beasts will we use to represent information (bits or q-bits)?
Today, in commercial applications, a bit is about 100,000 electrons,
give or take; or something like a 100,000,000,000,000,000 photons.  Yes,
we can manipulate individual electrons and photons in the lab.  In fact,
we can even in theory store multiple bits (as many as 900 "pixels" has
been proposed) with a single electron.



