In article <33316EC9.41C67EA6@latoso.cheme.cornell.edu>,
Jeffrey Potoff  <jpotoff@latoso.cheme.cornell.edu> wrote:
>> 
>
>For around $50K you can get 4 500MHz Alphas (Unix), well equiped, from
>one of the clone vendors.  Hook them up over a high performance switch
>and run hpf.  

This can be a very cost-effective approach for certain specific
jobs, but is not necessarily a good general-purpose number-crunching
environment.

First, you have the problem that the memory is statically partitioned
between the boxes.  So if you need to use more memory than the max in
any one box, you are forced to write parallel (message-passing) programs.
(HPF does hide the message-passing from you, if you can find a vendor
that will sell you an HPF over MPI compiler for these boxes).

Second, you must have relatively coarse grain parallelism to make
this worthwhile.  Multiple independent job streams work fine.  But
if you need tight synchronization, then you may have trouble.  All
the SMP machines listed will let you access any memory location in
~500 ns or less, while the networked version will require at least
200-300 times as long to pass a one word message (if you have a good
protocol stack -- otherwise it could be worse).  Similarly, the
SMP machines will support full bandwidth to all memory locations
(~400 MB/s sustained on the SGI, worst-case), while a fast ethernet
solution is limited to ~12 MB/s.

So the COW (cluster of workstation) approach is certainly a feasible
one for certain specific applications, it carries a lot of other cost
due to the inflexibility of the hardware and programming model.

Have fun!
-- 
