Skip Dooley <sdooley@edge.bogus> writes:

>As an argument for Sun
>Just where did NFS and NIS come from?

In the case of NFS V3, much of the protocol was thrashed out at a
meeting in Massachusetts involving DEC Sun, and others. (I was at
Alliant Computer at the time).  The impetus was that DEC was working
on it's first Alpha release, and NFS was the only file system that was
limited to 32 bits.  Our primary interest in V3 was to add 64 bit
support.  Sun was probably more interested in fixing the V2 write
performance problems.  (As were we, but the 64 bit FS problem was
critical, and our write gathering algorithm was a big help in that
arena.)

If you look at the Usenix NFS V3 paper, you'll find it was written
by engineers at DEC, Sun and Network Appliance.  If you look at the
SPEC-SFS group, Connectathon, etc., you'll find a lot companies have
had input to NFS.

A lot of the V3 code we use came from a Sun prototype, but I'm sure
the similarities must be hard to find now.

Who was the first to ship NFS V3?  DEC - in Digital Unix V3.0.  And we
defaulted to V3.

>Expect no incompatibilities Sun->Sun

We recently had to deal with a Sun-DEC performance problem where the "fix"
was to delay a write reply from the DEC server.  The support people
were also able to reproduce it with a fast Sun server.  Weird problem.

>Dec->Sun NFS is probably OK, but NIS? or worse NIS+ expect trouble
>I'd say although not based on experience

I haven't heard of a NIS problem between DEC and Sun in ages.  We don't
talk NIS+ yet, so if that's important to you, lean on DEC's sales people.
