mccalpin@frakir.asd.sgi.com (John McCalpin) writes:

> In article <33316EC9.41C67EA6@latoso.cheme.cornell.edu>,
> Jeffrey Potoff  <jpotoff@latoso.cheme.cornell.edu> wrote:
> >> 
> >
> >For around $50K you can get 4 500MHz Alphas (Unix), well equiped, from
> >one of the clone vendors.  Hook them up over a high performance switch
> >and run hpf.  
>
> . . . (HPF does hide the message-passing from you, if you can find a 
> vendor that will sell you an HPF over MPI compiler for these boxes).

I think Jeff intentionally mentioned HPF specifically in connection with
Alpha Unix.  Digital is widely acknowledged to have the best-performing
HPF (it is part of Digital Fortran).  Digital's HPF does not run over
MPI--it runs over a native, customized message-passing layer.  Digital's
HPF performs approximately as well as well-tuned, hand-coded MPI.

> Second, you must have relatively coarse grain parallelism to make
> this worthwhile.  Multiple independent job streams work fine.  But
> if you need tight synchronization, then you may have trouble.

HPF is designed for jobs requiring synchronization.  A good HPF compiler
generally uses a "loosely synchronous" execution model, where
synchronization is done only when required by data dependency (i.e. no
barriers before and after loops).  The language is not designed for
independent job streams.

HPF is new enough that the borderline cases are not yet explored.
However, it is known for sure that excellent speed-up can be attained in
codes where a serious amount of interprocessor communication is needed.
To take a simple example, Gaussian elimination run with HPF across a
cluster can speed up linearly with the number of processors.  Serious
commercial codes (from oil-company reservoir codes to market simulation
codes for Wall Street trading houses) are currently being written in HPF.

In general, numerically intensive codes with data access patterns that are
somewhat regular are obvious candidates for HPF.  It is not yet known
precisely how complex the data access pattern must be before HPF becomes
impractical. 

>  All the SMP machines listed will let you access any memory location in
> ~500 ns or less, while the networked version will require at least
> 200-300 times as long to pass a one word message (if you have a good
> protocol stack -- otherwise it could be worse).  Similarly, the SMP
> machines will support full bandwidth to all memory locations (~400 MB/s
> sustained on the SGI, worst-case), while a fast ethernet solution is
> limited to ~12 MB/s.

Actually, with a Memory Channel (reflective memory) interconnect, latency
to a remote memory location is less than 10 usec, which is only about 20
times slower than a local memory access.  Sustained bandwidth is around 65
MB/s.
 
> So the COW (cluster of workstation) approach is certainly a feasible
> one for certain specific applications, it carries a lot of other cost
> due to the inflexibility of the hardware and programming model.

The difference between remote and local memory access is enough to require
some thought on the part of the programmer, and to rule out codes with
very irregular data access patterns.  Still, we have found that HPF
gets excellent speedup on a very wide range of applications.

-Israel Gale
 gale@hpc.pko.dec.com



