Robert Koehler wrote:
> 
> John Vottero (John@mvpsi.com.nospam) wrote:
| : And how fast does electricity travel?
| 
| Well, electrons travel fairly slowly,

Remember that the electrons are always moving around anyway, due to
thermal effects.  When there's a current flow, *on average* they are
drifting more in one direction than the other.  However, an electron in
a conductor doesn't go far - a few atomic distances - before it hits an
atom.  "Fairly slowly" is an understatement - I recall typical numbers
in the foot-a-second range.

(On the other hand ... one of the triumphs of Special Relativity is that
it explains magnetism in terms of Columb - electrostatic - attraction. 
When you work out the mathematics, magnetism is there because of
relativistic transformations between the lab frame and the drift
velocity frame.  The effects are *tiny*, but they add up because there
are so *many* electrons moving.)

|				        but the wavefront of a voltage 
| change travels at the speed of light in the conductor, which is 
| slightly less than 186,214 miles/sec.  Perhaps likely the reference is 
| to metric units, about 3E8 meters/sec.

It's quite a bit slower than that - maybe .5 to .6c.  If you're talking
about moving through something with substancial inductance, it can be
much lower than that.

Light propagating through a fiber is going quite a bit below c, too.

However, lightspeed delay isn't the limiting factor on most designs
today - and where it is, using optical fiber probably won't help, since
it'll buy you at most a factor of 2.  The promised advanges of optical
computing are rather different:  Inherent insensitivity to noise, much
greater ease of cross-connects, extremely high bandwidth, truely massive
parallelism.  Some of this stuff works today; some is clearly on the
way; some is in the dream stage.
							-- Jerry



