In article <5gpmul$2llg$2@news-s01.ny.us.ibm.net>,
Stefan A. Deutscher <sad@utk.edu> wrote:
>
> I need some advice from the number crunching community again: My
>birthday is coming up and my girlfriend wants to get me a real computer
>in the 70k$ range [...] Now I am a bit at
>a loss at what is most advisable for heavy number crunching (floating
>point as well as large scale matrix stuff). 

Even if I was not employed by SGI, I would recommend that you look
into the Origin 200 series from Silicon Graphics.  

For a starting price of under $42k (US list price), you can get a
4-cpu machine with the outstanding FP performance levels:

	machine	      cpus   SPECfp_rate95
	Origin 200	4	 446
	DEC 2100A-5/375	4	 438
	HP K460		4	 398
	IBM RS/6000-595	1	~160       (est from 152 @ 120 MHz)
	Data Gen AV4900	4	 127       (4 166 MHz Pentium Pro's)

I have no idea how the other machines will price out relative to
the SGI, but I would be really surprised if the 4-cpu versions were
anywhere near as inexpensive.

(To be fair, I have chosen what appear to be very aggressive machines
from our competitors -- I don't know their machines well enough to know
if these are the most natural comparisons, but they are the fastest
small machines that I could find in the SPEC tables. I could not find
a faster Pentium Pro machine listed, but you can tell from the results
above that it will take a lot more than the 166 MHz to 200 Mhz upgrade
to fix this performance shortfall.)



>I would need not just a
>solid OS that can handle huge hard disks

Don't forget 64-bit support, so that you can easily work with
large files and large data.  Only SGI and DEC offer this now.


>but also good development tools, namely f77 and f90 compilers etc.
>(I might even want NAG lib / IMSL / ESSL on the box if that is available,
>does anybody know the prices off-hand for these machines?)

SGI just bought Cray Research, so you know that we are wholeheartedly
committed to the number-crunching market.  NAG and IMSL software is
available, and we are rapidly moving numerical library support and 
advanced compiler and O/S technology from the big Cray systems to
the Origin line.
