In article <33306AF8.565D@smarts.com>, Jerry Leichter <leichter@smarts.com> writes:
> It's quite a bit slower than that - maybe .5 to .6c.  If you're talking
> about moving through something with substantial inductance, it can be
> much lower than that.

Right about the reduced speed, but as far as I know, wrong about inductance.

Generally, reduced field propagation rate, or velocity factor, is attributed to
dielectric constants that are above those of free space.  It may be possible that
an inductor with a magnetic core would have reduced field propagation rates as
well, but without a magnetic core the usual dielectric constant calculations apply.

An inductive source driving a resistive load will result in increased rise times
and therefore apparent increased propagation delays.  Make everything current driven,
or replace the resistive load with a pure inductance, and you don't see the delay.

Far off topic, and the effects of inductance on propagation speed don't really have
an impact on the rest of our discussion anyway - but I thought I would mention it.

