        9GB, 3 1/2" drives _are_ available from Digital.  I just checked
    the Storageworks  home  page, http://www.storage.digital.com/swrks/,
    today  and  sure enough, there's an announcement of the 9GB  drives.
    The part number is DS-RZ40-VA, by the  way.   Got  that  by  calling
    1-800-STORWORK.

        Now, the _reason_ it's taken  so  long  for Digital to get these
    out,  as  related at last November's DECUS in Anaheim: the  supplier
    initially selected couldn't make drives that worked!   Digital  then
    had  to go back and try to select a different vendor, and go through
    all the qualifications, etc., again,  so  that set them back several
    months.   As another person said in an unrelated post, "Alphas  melt
    disks", so we should all be grateful to Digital  for  going  through
    this qual process rather than shipping disks that fail...

        Third  point:  the  current   family   of  3  1/2"  Storageworks
    containers  (SSB's)  can handle 18 watts of heat load (I think  I've
    got that right).  The just announced 7200 rpm, 9GB drives will weigh
    in just below 18 watts and may be used in those Storageworks shelves
    that have the extra fan capacity.  That is, if your shelf can handle
    an RZ29B (7200 rpm, 4.3GB) then it'll handle the 9GB drives as well.

        What we won't  see,  because  of  heat  load,  will be 10000 rpm
    versions of the 9GB drives, not in the current Storageworks shelves.
    There  was  a  non-disclosure presentation, which I can't  tell  you
    about, :-) but which  demonstrated  that  Digital  _will_  have  the
    technology to use the higher speed, higher power drives, just not in
    the existing shelves...

        Lastly, the RZ40 is only  available  in fast-narrow, at least at
    the moment.  I didn't ask if a fast-wide version was in the works (I
    think  it  is)  because  I need the narrow  version  anyway  for  my
    particular system.

