Another option is the 'toaster' style devices -- NFS appliances such as
the Falcon FastFile Pro or the Network Appliance.  I've been using a 
FastFile Pro with multiple full duplex 100mbit segments between Suns
and PC's running Solaris x86, and its performance is awesome. Its
also extremely reliable, with almost no performance hit during RAID
rebuilds.  It's a solid box. I've also heard good things about NetApp.

BOth Falcon and Netapp also support CIFS for NT sharing, so if you begin
to migrate to NT or you just want to add NT file sharing, you can add
the software onto the appliance and then share files betweem UNIX and NT
seamlessly. You could do this with Samba on UNIX, but in my experience 
the appliance is a cleaner, better performing, more reliable solution.
The multi-os capabilities of the Falcon were an issue for us in case
of future NT deployments.

-Al

