In article <3327944D.6ADA@EasyInternet.net>,
Paul DeMone  <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> wrote:
>Chris Ruemmler wrote:
>[snip]
>> 
>> I tried your program on a HP K460 1-way (180Mhz PA-8000) and
>> get 260 Mflops.  Given that the processor is running at 180Mhz, this
>> is 1.4 flops/cycle which is much better than the 500Mhz Alpha
>> is doing (0.35 flops/cycle).
>
>  You got that backwards.  The PA-8000 has to get that kind of per
>  clock performance to be competitive because its clock frequency is so
>  limited by its trailing edge 0.5 um CMOS process and off-chip caches.
>  The price of this approach is the need to implement two 128 bit wide
>  off-chip caches running at 180 MHz and more than a thousand signal and
>  power connections requiring a chip-on-board packaging/system design.
>
Again clock frequency is not everything.  Look at the IBM Power2.  This
was the best performing technical workstation for years yet it only ran
at about 66Mhz-77Mhz.  It blew the doors off the competition for most
technical applications.  It was also very expensive to make, but that 
does not matter so much if the chip excels as it did.

If your clock frequency is 500Mhz, but your design does not allow you
to overcome memory latency (as is the case with the 21164), then you
have problems.  Now if your problem fits in the small on-chip caches
of your 500Mhz processor (or in the register set), then the Mhz processor
will win.  In real life code, however, most code does not fit in such a 
small working set so you need to find ways to overcome the latency (such
as out-of-order execution and non-blocking caches with multiple outstanding
misses).  The 21164 does not do a good job at this, but it looks like
the 21264 will do better. 

>  BTW, HP apparently is starting to see the light of the Alpha approach,
>  supposedly the upcoming PA-8500 is going to have monstrous on chip
>  caches ;-)
>
Actually I think DEC is starting to see the light of the HP approach
with the 21264!  If you look at the design, it looks a lot like the 
PA-8000 (out-of-order,larger 2 cycle latency caches, lots of rename 
logic, multiple execution units, multiple load/store pipes).  I think
you have things backwards :).
  
--Chris
My own views no HPs




