Yes, we do plan to support MS language extensions which make sense
on both UNIX and OpenVMS.  It is the same compiler, after all.  In fact, if
you try many of them on the currently released F90 compilers (V4.1 on UNIX,
V7.1 on OpenVMS), you'll find that they work (though we don't support them
at present and we may have fixed problems since those versions were released.)
The whole idea is that there is ONE language definition which is supported
over a broad range of platforms, making it easy to scale your applications,
without recoding, from a PC up to our largest Alpha systems - "From laptops to 
teraflops", we like to say.

However, we don't have plans to port the Microsoft Developer Studio environment
to other platforms (and neither, as far as I know, does Microsoft.)  As for
the debugger specifically, I find that decladebug on DIGITAL UNIX and
the OpenVMS debugger have more capabilities than the DevStudio debugger and
are almost as "graphical" (more so in some cases.)
