In article <3328F78A.2494@cao.mts.dec.com>,
Peter Mayne  <Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> wrote:
>Chris Ruemmler wrote:
>> I tried your program on a HP K460 1-way (180Mhz PA-8000) and
>> get 260 Mflops.  Given that the processor is running at 180Mhz, this
>> is 1.4 flops/cycle which is much better than the 500Mhz Alpha
>> is doing (0.35 flops/cycle).
>
>Can you please define exactly what "much better" means here? Why is 1.4
>flops/cycle "much better" than 0.35 flops/cycle?
>

1.4 flops/cycle is "much better" than 0.35 flops/cycle in terms of
processor utilization.  The 21164 is a 4-way superscalar design that
can execute at most 2 flops/cycle peak, but only gets 0.35 flops/cycle
on this test.  The PA-8000 is also a 4-way superscalar design but can 
obtain a peak Mflops of 4 flops/cycle and gets 1.4 on this test.  So the 
21164 is getting 18% efficiency out of its core while the PA-8000 is at 
35% effeciency.

Another way to look at it is that the PA-8000 getting much more work
done out of each "tick" of Mhz than the Alpha.  I imagine there are
a LOT of "ticks" that go by on the 21164 at 500Mhz where zero work
is retired due to stalls waiting on memory. 
  
It would be interesting if someone posted results for a IBM Power 2
(or Super Power 2), I imagine these might get even better results than 
the PA-8000 in terms of efficiency, even though they have slower
clocks (77Mhz and 135Mhz).




