On Fri, 14 Mar 1997 17:00:27 +1000, Peter Mayne
<Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> wrote:

>Chris Ruemmler wrote:
>> I tried your program on a HP K460 1-way (180Mhz PA-8000) and
>> get 260 Mflops.  Given that the processor is running at 180Mhz, this
>> is 1.4 flops/cycle which is much better than the 500Mhz Alpha
>> is doing (0.35 flops/cycle).
>
>Can you please define exactly what "much better" means here? Why is 1.4
>flops/cycle "much better" than 0.35 flops/cycle?
>-- 
>PJDM
>Digital Equipment Corporation (Australia), Canberra, ACT
>-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
>These are my opinions, and have nothing to do with Digital.
>This was typed by an infinite number of monkeys.

A flop is a term that means floating point operation.  That is, an
operation on a floating point number.  Mflop is a mega flop. Some
processors have hardware floating point processors and others have to
emulate the operation in software. The term MIPS is also used to rate
computers.  A machine that can do x MIPS means that it can do x mega
instructions per second.  Two machines that have the same MIPS rating
may have a different flop rating. GUI based systems do a lot of
floating point operations and the flop rating one important
specification to consider when choosing a system.

