hedrick@geneva.rutgers.edu (Charles Hedrick) writes:

> I just tried a 167 MHz Sun Ultra 1, and got 61 Mflops.  This is with
> code I just got, so presumably it has the newest RISC optimizations in
> it.  These are not current machines: there are now 200 and 250 MHz
> Ultras available.  Compiled with Sun's C compiler version 4.0, -fast.

Try with

`-fast -xO5 -xsafe=mem -xtarget=ultra1/170 -xarch=v8plusa -xrestrict'

and see it jump to 80 MFlop (on the same machine).  The original
version comes out at 72 MFlops.  Some compilers are better than others
and I found the compilers for Alpha always seem to need more nudging.
But that's beside the point.  The code is really ugly (sorry
Mr.Henley).  I couldn't find out for instance how to drop in CGS
instead of CG (and I have implemented both before).  Is this a
nonlinear CG procedure?  And the matrix_matrix_multiply makes me
dizzy.  It looks more like a cross product or where are the minusses
coming from?  FORTRAN in C, so why don't write it in FORTRAN and get
the better optimizations F77 compilers are entitled to?  BTW, this is
an linear algebra benchmark:


 %Time Seconds Cumsecs  #Calls   msec/call  Name
  15.3  142.91  142.91  513056      0.2785  matrix_matrix_multiply
  15.0  140.26  283.17  237792      0.5898  calc_UV_sub_UdagV_add
