Your comments, of course, are interesting, but no need to display your
ignorance. The minuses come from the fact that the matrices are
SU3 matrices, and therefore complex. Do you understand what a complex
matrix is? Did you run the routines that Robert Harley rewrote? And as 
for the CG routine, it is not a typical conjugate gradient routine. 
The "matrix" that is inverted is not really a matrix, ok? So your
previous
experience with CG is completely irrelevant. The matrix multiplication
is the most expensive step in the conjugate gradient (as you will
agree),
but the actual multiplication is an abstract one in this sense. It
involves
transporting 8 neigboring complex vectors (ie, 6 components, 3 real, 
3 imaginary) to each point of a distinct parity via the gauge links (a
la
General Relativity) which are COMPLEX 3 dimensional matrices. It is
a complex four dimensional operation that is difficult to implement.

Now the fact is that typically the code would be run with a smaller
quark mass, in which case the CG will have more iterations. If you
checked,
you will notice that the matrix_matrix_multiply routine is not called
from the CG routine anyway, so it is not as important in the really
tough simulations (i.e., small quark masses!). I want to use small
quark masses, because these correspond to the low energy hadronic states
(such as protons etc) that are more interesting to me. I simply
posted my code because I am interested in the comments of others,
and also I would like to see how different machines compare. 

Thanks for your timings, BTW, I'll add them to my list.

Henley



