Jon Wright writes:

>>>> Does  the alliance of DEC with Micro$oft mean that there will be no
>>>> DEC Fortran for OS/2 until Bill Gates resignes? I really would like
>>>> to have an industrial strength compiler of DEC quality for my 
>>>> favourite PC OS, but so far it seems that on the PC side only Watcom 
>>>> is really multiplatform (from DOS ... Win NT, OS/2, you name it).
>>>> 
>>>> Anything in the pipes?

>>> No - we just don't see OS/2 as a viable market.

>> I am curious about what makes the Windows 95/NT market more viable, and
>> the OS/2 market less viable than VMS and related markets.

> I should think thats an easy one to answer, WIN96/NT has a much larger
> user base than OS2.

The size of the user base isn't the major concern, but rather the
demographics of that user base.  For example, Windows 95 users may be
more numerous, but it's popular in the home, where I wouldn't expect
to find many FORTRAN customers.  I would expect to find FORTRAN customers
among Windows NT users, but those aren't as numerous as OS/2 users.

> Dec themselves make machines that run OpenVMS and
> unix, if they themselves didn't provide a solid fortran compiler for 
> thier own machines how many would they sell? 

Irrelevant to the question of whether the OS/2 market is viable, when
compared to the market for their own machines.

> Also IBM have their own xlf compiler
> and if they don't think its viable porting their own compiler to their
> own OS why should DEC think its a viable market?

How do you know what IBM considers viable?  Just because they haven't
done it doesn't necessarily mean they don't consider the market viable.
For example, IBM could have made a deal to leave the market to someone
else, sort of like they've done with Netscape (development of their own
Web Explorer was stopped).




