Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


On 1/27/97, I submitted an order for 5 Enorex 500s
to my purchasing department. According to the
Lucent purchasing organization, the order with
authorization to bill was Faxed to Enorex on
2/11/97. Somewhere in there, Enorex set a 
promised ship date of 2/22/97. On February 19th,
I received a call from someone from Enorex
named Tracy about the order and how to contact
the Lucent Purchasing department. To my knowledge,
they successfully  contacted by 2/20/97. (Why they
needed to talk to them, I don't know.)

When I called on 2/24/97 to see if the systems had
shipped, I was told that the order was not put into
their system until 2/21/97 and that because I wanted
128MB of RAM, the earliest they could ship to me
would be 3/3/97. Yes, at this point I was miffed. 
I had been following this order because I am attempting 
to set up a trial for an advanced natural language 
processing system and I needed the machines ASAP to 
keep to an aggressive schedule. Near as I can tell,
it took Enorex over a week to put the order
into their system.

When I called on 3/3/97, I was told that the machines
would ship out that day and should arrive on 3/5/97. 
On 3/5/97, I received 3 machines. All three seem
to be fine and booted with no problem. I decided to wait
a few days to see if the other 2 machines would
arrive. On Monday 3/10/97, I called Enorex about the 
remaining 2 machines. 

They asked me if I know the serial numbers of the machines
that they shipped me! They called back after I left
work and indicated that it would take 2 weeks to ship
the remaining 2 machines. However, if I was willing to
take 64MB machines, they could overnight them on Tuesday and
ship the rest of the memory in 2 weeks. I called and
requested immediate shipping of the remaining systems. 
This morning on my way to my office, I passed the shipping
and saw the boxes, so hopefully the last 2 machines will be
delivered today.

This whole experience has left me less than enthusiastic
about Enorex. One possibility is that their extensive
advertising campaign has swamped them beyond capacity. 
They indicated to me several times, that my order was
caught up in some change of their systems from offering
12 configurations to offering more than 30.

We are already committed to rollout of our 
product to 3-4 customers, each needing a minimum
of 3 to 6 Alpha machines. If we are successful with
the initial rollout, we would could see sales of
10 to 20 additional systems this year. This
kind of performance from Enorex has placed them
at the bottom of the supplier list!

As far as the machines go, our HMM speech model building
tools are running 2.75 times a PPro 200 out of the box 
and without spending any time tuning the compiler 
optimization. Our DSP coding guru is planning to hand
code the inner distance computation and the viterbi 
decoder inner loop. (On an ADI SHARC DSP, these loops 
are 4 and 6 instructions long respectively.) We are
hoping that by applying a little ASM code and putting
in a few cache fetch hints, we will achieve performance
at near the maximum allowed by the system architecture.

BTW, PC magazine, in their Mar. 25th edition, had an
article on future processors (p168 - 175). Of course,
they spend 90% of the article talking about future
Intel processors, but they do include a sidebar on RISC
machines. In the side bar, they talk about PowerPC,
MIPS, SPARC, HP PA, and the 21264 Alpha. The article 
only weakly acknowledges the ALPHA's current leadership
in the last sentence and fails to point out the attempt
to bring the price down even further with the 21164PC
chip.
-- 
