Subject: Timings for the lattice QCD benchmark on single CPU and SMP and MPP computers
Date: 1997/03/13
Message-ID: <33284124.3343@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 225239598
Organization: University of Minnesota
Newsgroups: comp.benchmarks,comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec,comp.os.ms-windows.nt.misc


Hi

Here are the results from my lattice QCD benchmark
that many people have run. Thanks for all the
input.

If you would like to get the code for yourself,
you can find it at:
http://tonys.ecology.umn.edu/QCD

(qcd.zip contains all the files)

Or ftp everything from:

ftp tonys.ecology.umn.edu
username: bench
password: mark

directory: QCD

(btw, feel free to improve it, and send it back to
me... :)

Here are some results.... I put the T3D result in
there, but I should mention a couple of things. 
The T3D code did not have the Robert Harley
routines that RISC CPUs love (the routines that
require zillions of registers).... The T3D is also
old technology now, I will get timings on a T3E
soon. I didn't include my CM5 results, because it
is not identical to this code. The CM5 would
top the list at 24000, but again, this is actually
pretty poor compared to the MPP computers of today!

These results are in Mflops/s. I counted adds and
mults as flops.... all the other memory transfers
in the scatter/gather and all the other overhead
do not contribute any flops. This is a very memory
intensive code, which benefits greatly from fast/
large cache etc. But when it comes down to it,
the flop rate is the important thing, at least for
me it is. So I think the code gives me a good
idea of how the different systems compare.

Cray T3D, 512 nodes                       :  10240

The following timings all used the same size
lattice:

                                             Mflops/s

HP K460 1-way (180Mhz PA-8000)            :    260
500MHz Alpha (21164)                      :    177
SGI Origin2000 (single CPU)               :    148
333MHz Alpha (21164)                      :    113
Dell 200Mhz dual ppro (256k cache)        :    109
266MHz Alpha (21164)                      :    101
233 MHz VMS/Alpha (21064A)                :     68
Same ppro2, but on single CPU             :     67 
P6-200/256K Asus P6NP5 Linux 2.0.18       :     67
Dell p133c (512k cache)                   :     34
Sun Ultra 1 143Mhz Solaris 2.5.1          :     32
Sun SS20/61 (SuperSPARC, 50/60, 1M+20/16) :     25
Cray T3D (1 node)                         :     24 
Vaxstation 4000/90, VMS 6.2               :     12
486DX2/66 Linux 2.0.29                    :      4
4 processor(40mhz) SUN 690 (1 CPU)        :      3
Sinclair ZX-81                            :      0

thanks

Henley



