Hi again,

I just wanted to redirect this discussion -- the focus seems to be
turning towards NT rather than Digital UNIX.

My point is that I would prefer to serve NFS to my SunOS/Solaris env
using a Solaris fileserver, but our company has a lot of momentum
purchasing DEC servers for both Sybase (UNIX) and PC desktop (NT) sides 
of the business. My concerns are that I need to insure compatibility
with Sun *today* -- not with whatever OS I'm running 1 or 2 years from
now; which seems to be the concern of those writing the checks.

So, if anyone has good technical reasons that I can use for amo then
please post 'em here.  Thanks!

-AG

Another Geek wrote:
 
Good afternoon folks!
 
(oh, and I appologize for the wise crack Name and email address -- it so
I don't get any more mass mailings -- it's nice to know that our
technical
wizard counterparts respect our privacy!)

This is a solicitation for your opinions on Sun Solaris and Digital UNIX
for NFS serving. I'm trying to determine which is the superior choice
for
my environment. Well, actually I have a political battle on my hands,
and
although I'm an advocate for Sun, I'm trying to keep an open mind -- I
 need
to concern myself with all the technical issues that surround the intro-
duction of a new vendor into an up-til-now homogenious environment.
 
First off, I have to admit that I'm bias towards Sun... For two reasons
-- first, because my exposure to Digital's UNIX is next to none at all,
and second because most of my other equipment is Sun. In fact, we have
exclusively Sun systems, save for several DEC Alphas running Sybase, but
even these have been the domain of another Sysadmin, so I've not
. benefited(?)
. from the presence of these machines.
. 
. There is, at least in my organization, a new tendancy to inverst in
DEC
. for
UNIX, because of Digital's commitment to alternate operating systems,
such
as NT (and due to Sun's less-than-shinning track record in the area of
tech
support). A couple of years ago, when Sun was still shipping 70MHz cpus,
we
looked to DEC for their new Alpha in hopes to get better database
performance.
And with the popularity of NT, we now find ourselves trying to minimize
our
investments in hardware that we can't move to NT at a later date. I not
suggesting that everyone start investing in hardware based on OS
options,
 which may be unrelated to the current need or suitability, but this has
been
the argument made for DEC here at my company.
 
From my own standpoint (you may think this is irresponsibe and narrow
minded)
I don't want to make the personal investment of learning the nuances of
another
UNIX OS. I've been working with SunOS for over 7 years, and now consider
myself a SunOS/Solaris guru -- but as we all know, various flavors of
UNIX
have extremely different implimentations.

I think the most important consideration is that of meshing of one brand
of
UNIX with another.  This particular NFS server will be providing service
to
a medium-sized community of Sun servers (about 60 hosts), and will be
the
primary server for User accounts, etc., in an multi-NIS domain
environment.
So my biggest concern is not so much 'can DEC NFS server to Sun hosts',
but
what are the technical obsticles that stand in the way of everyday
issues.
Such as:

  - NFS is a widely used and standardized protocol. But will support
 issues
     come up when we mix platforms (each vendor pointing fingers).

  - Are there any technical issues with NIS (my biggest fear) -- how
well will
     DEC UNIX provide through and through NIS compatibilty -- will any
of my
     current functionality be sacrificed? Will I be giving up the option
to go
      to NIS+ at a later date.
 
   - When new features are introduced (I'll be using Veritas Volume
 Manager),
      will DEC (or any other UNIX vendor for that matter) be unable to
 provide
      immediate support for it.>
 
   - Sun has a certain level of commonality -- can anyone argue that Sun
      ins't the platform that more freeware applications are distributed
 with
      precompiled binaries.
 
 These are just some questions that come immediatly to mind; I'm sure
 there are
 many others. What I'm looking for is some concrete knowledge of why I
 should
 (or shouldn't) use DEC for NFS in an exclusive Sun environment (20%>
SunOS, 80%
 Solaris 2.x). I guess in a way I'd prefer to get are technical reasons
 why we
 shouldn't use DEC -- but fair is fair -- if anyone has equally good
 reasons
 why I shouldn't be concerned about mixed vendors (or DEC specifically)
 then
 by all means, that's appreciated also.
 
 Thanks in advance.
 -JAZ



