In article <1997Mar9.202419@dax>, wade@dax.asub.arknet.edu writes:
> I have a VAXStation 4000-60.  It had 16MB of ram.  I just added a
> 32Meg expansion.  The startup shows 48MB and a show config at the 
> prompt shows 48MB with all the right values in the slots.  (8MB in
> the first 2, 32MB in the second).  After boot, however, VMS still
> shows only 16MB.  

        Er, this is getting to be an FAQ topic...

        You _must_, when you add memory, AUTOGEN in order for the system
    to access it all.  Two ways to go about it:

        1) _If_ you had known ahead  of time, you _could_ have added
           a  line  MEMSIZE=98304  to  SYS$SYSTEM:MODPARAMS.DAT  and
           AUTOGEN'd, then shutdown, added  the  memory,  and  you'd
           have been all set on reboot;

        2) Having not had  the  forsight  (or otherwise RTFM'd), you
           now  need  only AUTOGEN and reboot.  On reboot, all  your
           system's memory will be available to VMS.

    The value 98304, above,  is  the  number  of 512-byte pages in 48MB.
    Since  you didn't do (1), _don't_ add MEMSIZE to MODPARAMS.DAT; that
    is only needed, and should only be done, when  you  want  to  resize
    your  system's  memory  and  AUTOGEN  prior to actually changing the
    memory (thus saving the extra reboot).  That AUTOGEN-specific symbol
    should not normally be present in the file...

        -Ken
