I think that Mr Palmer should also be told that a serious IT firm should
not try to quicken the pace of progress by killing off older products
and adopting what SEEMS to be to future. It is ok to adopt new stuff,
but there is no need to quicken the demise of stuff you THINK will not
last more than 10 years.

I beleive that Digital erred big time in trying to accelerate the move
from VMS to UNIX and (now to NT). Digital should have been there and
available for its customers and not tried to set trends. 

Yes, VMS will, one day, die off. Yes, that day may be within the next 10
years. But it is not a reason to tell customers that. Digital should
strive to lenghten VMS' life as much as possible by making it cost
competitive with newer platforms and letting evolution make customers
move on their own schedule/needs instead of reacting to Digital's hins
to move away from VMS.

10 years is a very long time in IT terms. It is a darn shame that
Digital has decided to quicken the death of VMS  when VMS could have had
quite a few more good years of high quality life. Considering that the
popular systems are striving to become VMS-like with clustering,
networking etc, VMS isn't that far off from what folks want in features.


Digital should have gone out of their way after the change in management
to ensure that Digital was committed to making VMS a cost effective and
highly performant platform. They may not have prevented Microsoft from
eventually dominating the market, but would have given VMS's a much
better and longer lifetime until it finally withers. And it would have
prevented the accelaration  or even reversed the move away from Digital
that has cause so many poor financial results.



