cz4xb1@exse01.eds.com wrote in article <858024286.989@dejanews.com>...
> I want to cluster 2 NT 4.0 servers both running on Compaq 5000R
platforms.
> I would like to use the DEC solution for NT clustering (V 1.1) but I am
> unsure wether or not it will work on Compaq 5000 servers.
> 
> Compaq has it's own clustering product, but the DEC product is a much
> better solution.
> 
> Does anyone know if I can use the DEC clustering solution on Compaq
> equipment?

Note that Digital certifies/tests certain systems, but the clustering
software/hardware per se has no device/vendor dependencies; i.e., for
the SCSI boards you should be able to use any differential SCSI boards,
that you can configure to work, and the software doesn't check that
it is running on a Prioris or AlphaServer (or at least it didn't use
to ;-).

Of course, it is simpler to buy a "clustering kit" complete with boards,
cables, software (and preferrably an external RAID enclosure) from
Digital (or a Digital reseller).

However, if you want support from Digital, then it would be best to
use a Digital server, because that's what Digital has tested.

And, if you ask me (I know you didn't, but I'll say it anyway ;-), I'd 
take a Prioris 6000 any time before any Compaq (besides, Compaq's 
on-line standby recovery server is no clustering solution, and the 
Tandem ServerNet has only recently been finished [says the press] - even 
if a Compaq presentor at the Microsoft Tech*Ed 96 in Nice, France last 
June claimed Compaq selected the ServerNet technology because it was
"proven technology" and I think he even mentioned "de facto standard",
which is quite funny given that it took many months from the date of
the presentation for the product to be released; and Compaq hasn't
still anything based on ServerNet that it is actually selling yet :-)


