Larry Kilgallen <kilgallen@eisner.decus.org> wrote in article
<1997Mar6.102056.1@eisner>...
> 
> Have you tried using VEST and specifying only emulation (not
> translation) ?  That has worked for me, and performance truly
> meets your specification of "no matter how slow(ly
> 
> So which of these is the problem you have encountered, and
> how many Alphas would you have bought ?
> 
DecMigrate & Vest have restrictions regarding absolute page sizes, global
memory usage, condition handling, and data field alignment. Let's say I've
got a 15 million line Cobol/Pascal/C application with 3 of the 4 problems
installed in 60+ locations. A conversion effort is mostly tedious work -
about like a year 2000 conversion effort. I might also have to seriously
consider RDB/RDO to Oracle/SQL and Decnet to TCP/IP conversions at the same
time. Since VAX code will not compile on Alpha & vice-versa, I would need
to maintain dual maintenance & support environments, plus 2 versions of the
code until all sites were converted(let's say 2 years). Considering all the
effort & expense plus all the questions regarding the future of VMS, one
would have to seriously consider moving to another platform/OS instead.

If there were a true VAX emulator, all the hardware could be upgraded first
- then a 'critical' software conversion could be done for performance
issues, with the rest happening over a period of time.   




