> > time. Since VAX code will not compile on Alpha & vice-versa, I would need
> 
> With higher level languages like C you can have the same code that will
> compile both on VAX and on ALPHA.  I would expect the only time you
> would have the above problem would be with MACRO code.
> 
For most programs that's true but you still must maintain separate object &
executable libraries. Each version must be tested & distributed in it's own
environment. In this case the SQL, TCP/IP, and data alignment changes would
require separate source libraries, plus some other things this app does are a
bit more complicated. Someone via Email suggested running DECNET over IP, but
that doesn't really address the problem of the eventual obsolescence of
DECNET.

We've studied the issues quite well & even test 'Vested' some code with a
copy of DECMigrate we borrowed from a consultant. DEC has been aware of the
issues since the early Alpha days - the only thing they've offered is a $40K
analysis. 

It's always amazed me that they have insisted on charging for this software
until very recently. Anyone else would have been giving CDs away on every
street corner from day 1.




