Norm Lastovica <lastovica@hotrdb.oracle.com> wrote in article
<5fplqd$o5q@nedc.us.oracle.com>...
> 
> 	Why would you want (or need) to support two code bases?  

> 	I would expect that most applications that are Cobol/Pascal/C
> would have very little work required to change the application proper
> in order to compile/link/run on an Alpha system.  
> -- 

The changes are not that difficult - it's the sheer size of the application
which makes it a large effort - just finding all the changes is not
insignificant. System test & tuning, especially with a new database, would be
a major effort in itself.

Two code bases would be required as it would not be practical for many
reasons to convert all 60+ sites to Alphas in less than about 2 years.
Attempting RDB/RDML/DSRI to Oracle/SQL and DECNET to TCP/IP(within the
application i.e. Mailboxes to Pipes etc.) conversions on the VAXes is a
possibility, but represents even more effort. Even if the source code could
be made common, the object libraries & executables have to be built, tested,
debugged, tuned, and distributed separately. 

Finally, when all is said & done, we've added very little business value for
the resources spent plus we're still on VMS - with limited options for adding
packaged applications and DEC hinting at obsolescence. I refuse to buy into
the DEC VMS-NT Affinity 'line' until someone from DEC tells me I can install
and run a native Intel/NT packaged application(including it's GUI admin
interface) on VMS - somehow I don't think that's going to happen - heck DEC
can't even get Pathworks to fully co-operate with NT 4.0. Few packaged NT
apps come with Alpha versions, I would expect even less with a VMS version.
NT itself has been dropped for the PowerPC due to lack of sales - the same
could also happen with Alpha. I like VMS, but unfortunately that's not a very
good basis for making a business decision for a mission critical application.
BTW - I'm not convinced that Microsoft fully understands corporate IS either!
:)

Opinions are my own etc. etc. 



