> I would like to know, how does these values compare to official?

I don't think so... one would expect the alpha to beat
the ppro.

I think my code is especially sensitive to fast cache / memory, because
it is an example of code for which a pentium pro beats a fast alpha!
The conjugate gradient used involves many multiplications and 
additions of very small (3 dimensional) complex matrices and vectors.
The vectors used in the calculation are also gathered and scattered
in four dimensions, and the ratio of communication/computation is
quite high too. The other thing perhaps is the use of single 
precision... would this make a difference for the alphas? I have
not tried coding different versions of the basic numerical routines,
but it would be very interesting to get the opinion of others 
before I do so.

I am hoping that more people will run it on their fast machines (people
who know which compiler switches to use to really make the code sing)
and their pentium pros and send me their timings. Perhaps a fast
alpha with a very large L3 cache would do well.

If anyone else would like the source code, please send me email.
The NT version will run on a quad CPU machine too... Does anyone out
there have a eight-CPU box?? (Is there such a thing?). If you do,
my program would love to start 8 numerical worker threads on it!! 

cheers

Henley



