In article <01bc2a33$7a8dbfe0$610258aa@alsnt>, "Al Kral" <KralA@CTT.com> writes:

> I do hope that Digital's assistance in moving from VMS to NT will be better
> than their assistance in moving from VAX to Alpha. It continues to amaze me
> that they have still not come up with an emulator that would run ALL VAX
> executables unmodified on Alpha - no matter how slow. I wonder how many
> Alpha sales that would generate?

Have you tried using VEST and specifying only emulation (not
translation) ?  That has worked for me, and performance truly
meets your specification of "no matter how slow(ly)", which
is the reason, I believe, the capability is not an advertised
feature of VEST.

That technique will not work for images linked prior to VAX
VMS V4.0 (1985 or so) because DEC made a tradeoff regarding
how far back people cared (and image headers changed at that
time).

VEST will not work at all with Ada images, since DEC decided
that Ada use was exclusively in shops that do not lose their
source.

VEST and TIE will not work with VAX images linked against
_recent_ versions of VMS (essentially those released after
Alpha versions of the corresponding compilers were available).
One can always link against libraries from older versions of
VMS, back to 4.0 which is the VEST limit anyway.

So which of these is the problem you have encountered, and
how many Alphas would you have bought ?

I believe that even the pervasive MacOS 68K emulation on PowerPC
will not handle programs which are not "32 bit clean", limiting
the time range of that emulation to less than the range of VEST
and TIE on Alpha VMS.

Larry Kilgallen



