Hello all,

  We recently had a DEC AlphaStation become available here.  It used to run
VMS, but once the need for that disappeared, we decided to switch to Linux. We
ordered and installed RedHat Linux 4.1 for Alpha, and things have been going
pretty well.  However, setting up X windows has proved to be an incredible
bear.  I think custom modelines are needed, mainly because the "standard" ones
included are way off base.  :) 

  SuperProbe is useless (doesn't know about Alpha's, apparently).  We got the
XF86_TGA server that came with RedHat to start after some tinkering, but we
can't get it to use the monitor in a decent video mode.  We would like to do
1280x1024 with a good refresh rate (something we know the hardware is capable
of).  Right now, I can either have X start and throw the monitor out of sync,
or (using a different Modeline) have it run at a ridiculously low refresh
rate.  I've tried reading VideoModes.doc, but I get lost about when he starts
talking about sync pulses.

  If anyone can help me, either with some sort of dumbed-down version of
VideoModes.doc, or pointing me someplace else, or anything, I would *really*
appreciate it.  System specs follow; if I can provide any other information,
please let me know. 

Machine: Digital AlphaStation 250 4/266
OS: RedHat Linux 4.1, kernel 2.0.27
Video adapter:
  DEC TGA video card with 2 meg RAM
  The XF86_TGA server had this to say about it:
    DEC 21030 TGA 8 Plane Chip Found.
    Using ICS1562 programmable clock
    Maximum allowed dot-clock: 135.000 MHz
Monitor: DEC VRC21-HA 21-inch multi-frequency monitor

  The monitor manual doesn't give frequency ranges, it only offers suggested
presets.  The one I'm interested in would be "1280x1024, 72.56 Hz vertical,
77.17 kHz horizontal".  I do have information which indicates the monitor is
capable of 50-152 Hz vertical and 30-94 kHz horizontal. 

  Thanks again for any ideas.  :)

