From: henley <henley@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>
Subject: A portable benchmark for ANSI C compilers, Windows NT and MPP supercomputers.
Date: 1997/03/06
Message-ID: <331F8BA9.4F96@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 223678943
Organization: University of Minnesota
Newsgroups: comp.benchmarks,comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec,comp.os.ms-windows.nt.misc


Hi

I have written a portable C code to perform calculations in
Lattice Quantum Chromodynamics. The benchmark I used consists
of 5 iterations of the Hybrid MonteCarlo method, and 
includes all the overhead associated with the full simulation.
The most expensive part of the code is a conjugate gradient
based iterative solver. The lattice used is 8^4, where
the quarks are represented by complex 3 vectors at the
lattice sites, and the gluons are represented by complex
3 dimensional matrices located on the links between lattice
sites. The communication patterns involve nearest-neighbor
interactions, in four dimensions.

The code is portable between any ANSI C compiler, the
Cray T3D (I haven't tried it on the T3E yet), and 
multi-threaded Windows NT. The Windows NT version can
have 1, 2, 4, or 8 threads for multiple CPUs. The T3D
version will work for any number of nodes that is a power
of two. The code is several thousand lines long. 

Dell 133Mhz Pentium, NT4.0     :    34 Mflops/s
Dec 333Mhz 21164, Digital Unix :    51 Mflops/s
Dell 200Mhz ppro (256k cache)  :    67 Mflops/s
Dell 200Mhz dual ppro          :   109 Mflops/s

(To be compared with 24 Gflops/s on the CM5 with an
earlier version of the code. I also achieved 10 Gflops/s on a
512 node T3D with the same code as the above simulations, with
the communications done using the SHMEM approach.)

I have put most of my effort into the algorithms, and not
into the numeric routines (yet). If you would like to run this
code on your computer (all it takes is the compilation of a
single file, such as cc -fast -tune ev5 -lm code.c etc, in the 
case of the alpha timings), please contact me. I'll collect the
results and post them. Also, I would very much like to see how
it runs on various multiple CPU windows NT boxes, especially
alpha based machines and quad CPU machines.

Thanks

Henley



