Jonathan Ridler wrote:

> .........
> My opinion based on what was (and was not) said:
> 
> Digital has decided that OpenVMS has little future in the computing world
> as they see it in the near future.  Digital will produce a few more
> OpenVMS "cutting edge" products and will then proclaim OpenVMS to be
> "mature".  Digital will then not invest in any further OpenVMS
> development.  Soon after, OpenVMS will be made a "maintenance only"
> product.  Existing customers will be looked after for a reasonable period
> (some years?), but new customers (if any) will be pure bonus.  Within a
> few years of the product "maturing", Digital will encourage OpenVMS sites
> to migrate to Windows NT. .......
 
I have to agree with the sentiments that Jonathan has expressed in his
two
recent posts. There are many of us feeling frustrated by the way Digital
are handling OpenVMS.

The problem is that DEC seem to want it both ways - they want to keep
existing customers who use VMS while at the same time telling/selling us
on what they see is their future.

But the future the way they see it is not necessarily the way the
customer sees it. My organisation may not be typical, but while it is
easy for
Digital to convince us that VMS has no future (they only have to hint!)
they have ** NO ** hope of ever convincing us to use other Digital
products (we
have another preferred vendor).

Any migration from VMS here will always be to a non-Digital platform!

At the moment Digital seem content to keep pouring the research dollars
into VMS and to LET customers use it if they choose - all the while
sending
us publications telling us how to migrate to other platforms and telling
us how good the alternatives are. Routinely we see information from
Digital which hints at the demise of VMS: and I would say the is the
best example
of a self-fullfilling prophecy that I have ever seen!

And yet recently in this newsgroup we see information about how VMS is
very likely to become at least an order of magnitude better at
transaction
processing than any other system. How do you think Digital will handle
this? My guess is that they won't want to alienate potential Unix or NT
customers so they will not tell anyone about their new technologies.
Instead in a couple of years, because sales are a disappointment, they
will
decide to adopt all their new ideas on some other platform.

In my opinion Digital really need to get out there and tell everyone
what they have got. In the past DECUS acted as a huge
information/marketing
organisation and everyone involved knew that Digital had fine products.
But now they are in the world of commodity systems and don't know how to
tell
us what they have! I wouldn't be surprised at the moment if the man on
the street thought that Digital have something to do with zoos!!

And I can't help wonder what the new Digital will be like? Will they
sell
PC clones and Unix and Microsoft products? Will the only differentiator
from
other companies be that they started bigger and build something called
an 'Alpha' which is like a Pentium? Where are they going? 

As for VMS the situation is made very hard by the fact that it is so
tightly coupled to Digital. If Digital want to kill it there is no-one
who can stop it!!! Perhaps customers can slow it by continuing to buy it
- but
a determined Digital will always win! You can bet that the world would
be a very different place now if Digital had either ported VMS to other
vendor
platforms, or even released the system binaries into the public domain
several years ago...    ain't that food for thought!

Paul Nankervis
paul@lin.cbl.com.au
All opinions expressed here are personal and do not necessarily
represent the views of my employer or even my friends!! :-)



