> > Dave Cherkus ------- UniMaster, Inc. ------ Contract Software Development
> > Specialties: UNIX Internals/Kernel TCP/IP Alpha Clusters Performance ISDN
> > Email: cherkus@UniMaster.COM  When the music's over, turn out the lights!
> No, I mean, if the cards are *really* PCI 2.1 compliant (which most are
> advertised as), then they should run fine at 66 (or whatever) MHz.

Ah, one other little complication is the fact that the 66 MHz
PCI connector has its notch in a different place.  Not just
because it's 66 MHz, but because 66 MHz PCI uses the
"3.3V signaling" flavor of PCI, which is incompatible with
the "5V signaling" version of PCI that everyone uses.

This means that unless you have a "3.3V signaling" PCI card (which
might not exist anywhere) or a "Universal" PCI card, you couldn't
even physically fit it into a 66 MHz PCI bus slot if you had one.

Regards,
Andy Ingraham



