Organization: DEC
Reply-To: everhart@star.enet.dec.com (Glenn C. Everhart)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



Perhaps (to throw a little gasoline on the fire here) :-) the problem with
lies in the market is that product liability isn't carried into this 
area. If Brand X says something that isn't so in its marketing, and
person A can show he relied on X's statement and was harmed by it, there
are possibly ways to sue X. There is a long tradition that "puffery" in
advertising is OK, which makes this kind of thing difficult. However
concrete examples, perhaps with ad agencies named as codefendants,
where the main issue was untruthfulness in the ads might go a long way
to discourage lies.

Then again, it might only increase the already way too costly litigation
in the US... 

Naah...too risky.

Censoring ads is something else again. Too many hidden agendas around
and unseen influences. You want YOUR company's ads to be censored by
someone who might be, er, too friendly with some competitor's folks?

Having people lying in the market is bad enough without risking the
lying getting done by people with the armed forces at their call.
(More than it is, that is... :-( ).

So much for philosophical digressions.



