In article <331AF5EB.75D0@sensor.com>, Ron Natalie  <ron@sensor.com> wrote:
>I probably should no the answer to this, but my memory is going.
>Why is it on the DEC unices, typing ^Z to an X application causes
>it to get blown off the server when this doesn't happen on other
>platforms (this predates the alphas too, it used to happen on
>Ultrix).
>-Ron

Does this happen when the DEC Unix machine is the client or server?
Does this happen to all applications?  Does this happen only with X
applications?

I myself have never seen this happen.  If a random user came up to
me with this problem, I'd ask them these questions:

1.  Which shell are you using?
2.  Do you set the interrupt character in your .login or shell startup
    script?  (i.e., a 'tset' or other terminal initialization command?)

If the answer wasn't obvious from either of the above, I'd begin
a serious inspection of the .login and .Xdefaults files.  All sorts
of cruft can live in these files, and often experienced gurus can't
even figure out how all the various commands and initializations
can interact with each other.  The first step of my 'serious inspection'
would consist of renaming the startup files to something relatively harmless
and logging in again such that they aren't executed at all.

Tim.



