The 64-bit race is   misleading, in that the definition of 64 bitness
and what qualifies as a 64 bit machine or app is not clear to the
novice consumer.   I hope that your article can assist in the clean up
of this over-simplified descriptor.  For example when a vendor claims
64 bits, are they talking about:  CPU registers?  CPU Cache? Data
Addressing?  Ability to compile apps for 64 bits?  ...or are the
vendors  trying to claim all of the above.    

All of the big Unix vendors have incorporated major enhancements in
the hardware in conjuction with their 64 bit offerings.  These
innovations should not be confused to be a result of the 64 bit
components.

An interesting table to see would contain, the major 64 bit
architectures and details on how 64 bit they are.

If such a table exists I would love to hear about it.

