On 28-FEB-1997 at 22:32:27 GMT, Sean Ennis <ennis@escape.ca> asked
> 
> I'm trying to find the layout of a vt220 terminal's keyboard.
> Not particularily the code, but where each key is on the keyboard.

> I need to use vt220 for a project I'm on, and the software I'm using 
> will emulate vt220.  Hopefully the keys are in the same general location.


The DEC VT220 terminal came equipped with the LK201 keyboard.

I keep a GIF diagram of an LK201 on the "Keyboard/Mice" subpage reached
from my "Video Terminal Information" web page at this URL:


    http://www.cs.utk.edu/~shuford/terminal_index.html


If you have not previously examined an LK201, you may be surprised by
certain aspects:

   - There is no Escape key (F11 works as Escape only in VT100 mode;
     you can however press Control-[ to generate an Escape code).

   - the F1 through F5 toprow function keys are local only and do not
     transmit any characters to the remote host. You do also have
     "Help", "Do", and F17 through F20 to play with, and there are
     the PF1 through PF4 keys on top of the numeric keypad.

   - Whereas on a PC/AT keyboard you have editing keys arranged thus

           Insert     Home        PageUp

           Delete     End         PageDown

     on the LK201 you have

           Find       InsertHere  Remove

           Select     PrevScreen  NextScreen

   - The < and > are on the same key at the controversial B00 position
     (left of the Z key, the position of which is called B0).

   - The "Compose Character" key is a great mystery.  :-)


When the VT420 came out, it was equipped with the LK401 keyboard, which
has the same basic layout. However, the VT420 allows the user to adjust
the configuration of the keyboard somewhat, to soften some of the above 
quirks. 

You don't say what terminal-emulation software you will be running.
Different products vary widely in how they address the key-position
differences.


