Ben,

Thanks for your comments, they are welcome and from it I get an idea of how
someone else sees my idea.

The Starion case seems to have been made as a "convertable" case, meaning
the back cut out is fixed with screws.  In checking the motherboard
dimension (roughly) it is AT sized.  The mounting screws appear to be in AT
relative spots although I'm going to have to caliper it to make sure.  All
that's required is a formed insert that re-orients the rizer cutouts back
to AT format and screws in, and I think the case is already dimensionally
correct for that.  Perhaps DEC's case supplier already fabs an alternate
configuration insert.  I'm working on this possibility.

Observing this is what gave me the idea that it might be possible.  Richco
makes adhesive board standoffs that can be used in the center AT screw
holes to provide mainboard support.  Actually many baby AT board makers
recommend all plastic and only one metal mainboard support anyway.

A kit would consist of the adaptor, the Richco supports, and a drilling
guide for the front edge supports only needed when using full length AT
expansion products (now rare).  Most people buying the kit would just
forget this step since most boards are now short PCI or Half ISA format. 
If the kit were sold in volume, the cost could be quite low, far lower than
I quoted.  I was testing the waters here to see if there is a market.  I'm
going to do a prototype for my own use anyway.

It's not that I can't buy a case.  I like hobbying with machines and like
to do these niche types of designs.  Note: I was the only person in town
with a AT&T PC6300 with a '286 mainboard installed years ago.  I loved it
because it had a piece of my design.  I'm attempting to do the same thing
here and save some folks the full cost of a new case.  Perhaps it will be
1/3 or 1/2 the cost of a new case and provide an attractive alternative.  

Jeff


